WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:12.720
US inflation hit a three-decade high in October&nbsp;
2021, rising by 6.2 percent. With little evidence

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.480
that inflation is moderating, each monthly&nbsp;
inflation print belies the Federal Reserve's view

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.640
that heightened price levels are but a transitory&nbsp;
phenomenon caused by supply chain problems. While

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.960
supply-side factors have played a role, the&nbsp;
Fed also has contributed importantly to rising

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.920
inflation by its continued pursuit of an ultra-easy&nbsp;
monetary policy. It has done so by its massive

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.292
bond purchases and by keeping interest rates at&nbsp;
their zero bound, despite the pick-up in inflation.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.360
And it has done all of this at a time when the&nbsp;
economy is receiving its largest peacetime

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.120
budget stimulus on record. Perhaps it's time for&nbsp;
the Federal Reserve to reconsider its monetary

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:02.000
policy stance. If not, it risks causing inflationary&nbsp;
expectations to rise, which could take us further

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.429
down the path towards another wage-price spiral.

00:01:07.391 --> 00:01:13.769
How has inflation impacted you? Please let us know
in our poll. Also let us know what other topics

00:01:13.769 --> 00:01:21.680
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and subscribe&nbsp;for more research and videos from AEI.

